[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A place logic can attach is a join point; a time-based one is a checkpoint

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-JOINT-NOT-SURFACE
TITLE: A place logic can attach is a join point; a time-based one is a checkpoint
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: The hook machinery ships under a different name in its published interface, or joint proves to collide with a term a consumer already uses.
STATEMENT: >>>
A place where logic COULD attach to the sdoc machinery is a JOIN POINT --
"joint" in conversation. A joint that fires on a moment in a workflow (a
commit, a push, a check) rather than on an operation over the canon is a
CHECKPOINT; a checkpoint is a kind of joint, not a second concept.

"Surface" is NOT used for this. The repository already means option surfaces
and grammar surfaces by it, and a third meaning would make every sentence
about the hook layer ambiguous.

The aspect-oriented split behind the term is kept whole: a join point is
WHERE, a pointcut is WHICH, and advice is WHAT. Advice is never part of the
hook machinery; it is what a higher layer attaches.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Ruled by the operator on 2026-08-28. Two candidates were on the table: the
operator's own placeholder "surface", and the industry term. The industry term
won because it arrives with a where/which/what split that maps exactly onto
the layering rule -- the machinery owns where, a higher layer owns what.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The operator asked that the term be reinforced and their own drift corrected
on sight, which INV-SETTLED-STAYS-VISIBLE now requires generally. AUTHORED_BY
reads llm because the writer has no flag for the other value.
<<<
